As my dataset grew over successive iterations, my understanding of the exploration–exploitation trade-off became more practical and less theoretical. Early on, I leaned more toward exploration, testing diverse regions of the search space because I had little information about where optimal values might lie. This often meant taking risks on uncertain query points. However, as more data became available, I shifted toward exploitation, focusing on regions that had already shown promising results. The balance wasn’t static—I still included occasional exploratory queries to avoid premature convergence, but these became more targeted rather than random.

With more feedback, my optimisation strategy became increasingly informed and efficient. Initially, feedback was sparse and noisy, so it was difficult to identify clear trends. Over time, patterns emerged, allowing me to make more confident decisions about where to query next. This process closely resembles how reinforcement learning agents update their Q-values: each new data point acts like a reward signal that refines expectations about different regions of the search space. As in RL, I gradually placed more weight on consistently high-performing areas while still allowing for occasional updates when new information contradicted earlier assumptions.

Relating this to the AlphaGo Zero case study, my approach reflects a simplified form of iterative self-improvement. While I wasn’t literally performing self-play, each round of querying and feedback acted as a cycle of learning and adjustment. My strategy evolved based on its own previous performance, becoming more refined over time. Overall, my approach was closer to model-free learning, since I relied heavily on trial-and-error and observed outcomes rather than building an explicit model of the underlying functions. However, in later stages, I did begin to anticipate where good results might lie, which introduces a light element of model-based thinking.

Reinforcement learning strategies could significantly enhance real-world optimisation tasks like this. For example, methods such as epsilon-greedy or Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) could systematically manage exploration, ensuring that uncertainty is reduced efficiently while still prioritising high-reward areas. Additionally, value-based methods like Q-learning could help track and update expected rewards for different regions, improving convergence speed. In more complex settings, model-based approaches could simulate potential outcomes before making costly real-world decisions, increasing both efficiency and safety.

Overall, this process demonstrated how balancing exploration and exploitation, leveraging feedback effectively, and iteratively refining strategies are all central to successful optimisation—both in this project and in reinforcement learning more broadly.
